GitHub's large user base and extensive repository database make it an ideal platform for conducting extensive software maintenance activities.  By taking advantage of automated tool support, maintenance tasks can be both initiated and executed by developers, leading to the creation of various maintenance types.  This research targets a specific aspect of this activity - meta-maintenance - which involves a set of distinct repository maintenance types sharing a common core.  To illustrate the potential of meta-maintenance, we developed a clustering approach on a dataset of 1.1M GitHub repositories, categorizing them into four types of repository maintenance.  The results show that about 5.3% of all GitHub repositories can be characterized by these maintenance types.  A significant proportion of these repositories - approximately 11.8% - exhibit identical files, suggesting that these repositories could be further maintained through meta-maintenance.  The outstanding promise of this research is to investigate other forms of meta-maintenance on GitHub, thereby optimizing repository maintenance workflows. 

Keywords: GitHub, software maintenance, meta-maintenance, clustering. 

